    "category": "logical",
    "scenario": "[LSER.17.2]: series `min` and `max` select under the order `pXX` sorts by, so of -0.0 and +0.0 `min` is -0.0 and `max` is +0.0 in whichever order the two arrived. Four `by:` identities on each of `all_time` (`MA`), `sliding 1_h` (`MW`) and `sample 8` (`MS`), three samples each, one per pump: m1 {0.0, 1.0, -0.0} and m2 {-0.0, 1.0, 0.0}, whose `min` is -0.0; x1 {0.0, -1.0, -0.0} and x2 {-0.0, -1.0, 0.0}, whose `max` is 0.0. Each identity's fourth assert (9.0) fires the schema's rule, which reads the three samples before it ([LSER.8.6]), one schema per pump. The value is read through its text ([LDET.3.2.7]), since `==` cannot tell the zeros apart. A runtime that keeps the older zero gets m1's `min` and x1's `max` wrong, one that keeps the newer gets m2's and x2's. PATH PINNED: libds `ds_series_signed_before` in `series/welford_ewma.c` (`all_time`), the trims of `series/monotonic_deque.c` (`sliding`) and `series/reservoir.c` (`sample`); Mercury `ds.series.extreme` through `signed_min` and `signed_max`.",
    "rules_ds": "use default\n\nschema MA scope logical:\n    k: String key\n    v: Float mutable\n\nschema MW scope logical:\n    k: String key\n    v: Float mutable\n\nschema MS scope logical:\n    k: String key\n    v: Float mutable\n\nseries MA.v (by: k):\n    min, max all_time\n\nseries MW.v (by: k):\n    min, max sliding 1_h\n\nseries MS.v (by: k):\n    min, max sample 8\n\nrule read_ma:\n    MA m where m.v > 5.0\n->\n    let lo = m.min ?? 99.0\n    let hi = m.max ?? 99.0\n    log info \"all_time {m.k} {lo} {hi}\"\n\nrule read_mw:\n    MW m where m.v > 5.0\n->\n    let lo = m.min ?? 99.0\n    let hi = m.max ?? 99.0\n    log info \"sliding {m.k} {lo} {hi}\"\n\nrule read_ms:\n    MS m where m.v > 5.0\n->\n    let lo = m.min ?? 99.0\n    let hi = m.max ?? 99.0\n    log info \"sample {m.k} {lo} {hi}\"\n",
